The '''Guasasa''' ({{IPA|bl|gwa'sasa}}) is the national cocktail of [[Balisca]], made with [[Aguardiente de Caña|''Aguardiente de Caña'']] (a distilled spirit from fermented [[W:Sugarcane|sugarcane]] juice), lime, and a local fruit (typically [[W:Guava|guava]] or [[W:Passion fruit|passion fruit]]), and ''[[Mel de Caña]]'' (unrefined cane syrup). The drink is prepared by blending the fruit and syrup, then adding the spirit and ice. Rooted in the culture of the [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] community, today the Guasasa is known and consumed across the confederation and is considered a quintessential element of [[Cuisine of Balisca|Baliscan cuisine]].

Due to its cultural importance, the Guasasa was declared an item of [[Bien de Interés Cultural|Intangible Cultural Heritage]] by the [[Confederal Congress]] in 2011. It is celebrated for its reflection of Baliscan principles, such as decentralization and the use of local, sustainable ingredients.

== Etymology and origin ==
The name "Guasasa" is popularly believed to be derived from the term "[[guaso]]," a name for the historical Maroon cowboys and freedom fighters who inhabited the Araucarian hinterlands during the [[Colonial Balisca|colonial]] and [[Maroon Confederacy|pre-confederation]] eras. The drink is said to have originated in the self-liberated communities of Araucaria, created as a rustic and potent beverage using the local distilled spirit and whatever fruits were on hand.

== Cultural significance ==
===Variations===
The core recipe of the Guasasa is more of a template than a strict rule, with several well-known regional variations:

'''Pindoramense Guasasa''': In the community of [[Pindorama]], the fruit used is typically [[W:Passion fruit|passion fruit]] (maracuyá) or [[W:Mango|mango]], giving the drink a sharper, more exotic flavor.

'''Highland Guasasa''': In the cooler, temperate highland areas, the drink is often made with muddled blueberries or even tart apples, resulting in a different flavor profile.

'''Guasasa Fresca''': A popular non-alcoholic version where the spirit is replaced with sparkling water or a local herbal infusion.

